  "Number": 14,
  "Question": "When simplified \\(\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{x^4-1}{2x^2}\\right)^2}\\) equals:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(\\dfrac{x^4+2x^2-1}{2x^2}\\)",
    "B": "\\(\\dfrac{x^4-1}{2x^2}\\)",
    "C": "\\(\\dfrac{\\sqrt{x^2+1}}2\\)",
    "D": "\\(\\dfrac{x^2}{\\sqrt2}\\)",
    "E": "\\(\\dfrac{x^2}{2}+\\dfrac1{2x^2}\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "E",
  "Solution": "The denominator requires \\(x\\ne0\\). For every such real \\(x\\), the radicand is positive. Combining its terms over a common denominator reveals a perfect square:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n1+\\left(\\frac{x^4-1}{2x^2}\\right)^2\n&=\\frac{4x^4+(x^4-1)^2}{4x^4}\\\\\n&=\\frac{x^8+2x^4+1}{4x^4}\\\\\n&=\\left(\\frac{x^4+1}{2x^2}\\right)^2.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe principal square root of a square is its absolute value. Since \\(x^4+1>0\\) and \\(2x^2>0\\), the quotient is positive, so\n\\[\n\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{x^4-1}{2x^2}\\right)^2}\n=\\frac{x^4+1}{2x^2}\n=\\boxed{\\frac{x^2}{2}+\\frac{1}{2x^2}}.\n\\]\nThis is choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{E}}\\), valid throughout the original domain \\(x\\ne0\\).",
